<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Communist Party of Vietnam</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1930">1930</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as CPV, Vietnamese Communist Party, Vietnam Workers' Party</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> is the ruling and only legal party of Vietnam. Founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh">Ho Chi Minh</a> in 1930 and soon renamed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochinese-communist-party">Indochinese Communist Party</a>, it led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and ruled North Vietnam as the Workers' Party from 1951. It took its present name in 1976 after reunification, and in 1986 launched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/doi-moi">Doi Moi</a> market reforms.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-vietnam.jpg" alt="Communist Party of Vietnam" width="320" height="279" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original work by Eureka287, vector work by Lasse Havelund, final edit by ComradeBlots · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Communist_Party_of_Vietnam_flag_logo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The party traces its history to 1925, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh">Nguyen Ai Quoc</a>, later Ho Chi Minh, established the Vietnamese Revolutionary Youth League. Its aims were to end French colonial rule, win national independence and redistribute land to working peasants, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-international">Comintern</a> supported the groundwork financially. In 1928 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> destroyed the League's headquarters in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangdong">Guangdong</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and the group was driven underground and split. On 17 June 1929 more than twenty delegates from cells in the Tonkin region met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a> and declared the League dissolved and a Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">Indochina</a> established. The League's other faction, based in Cochinchina, declared itself the Communist Party of Annam in late 1929.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1941 the party set up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> as a coalition front. In March 1945 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> overthrew the French colonial regime in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">Indochina</a>, and by August Japan had surrendered. The Viet Minh used the power vacuum to launch a nationwide uprising, and Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bao-dai">Bao Dai</a> abdicated on 25 August. Ho Chi Minh proclaimed the independence of the Democratic Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> on 2 September 1945, but no country recognised it at first. The party led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-indochina-war">First Indochina War</a>, after which it became the ruling party in the north in 1954.</p><p>The third party congress, held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a> in 1960, set the task of building socialism in North Vietnam and committed the party to the liberation of the south. After the party took power in the whole country in 1975, it unified Vietnam as the Socialist Republic in 1976. It had run a command economy in the north since 1954 and introduced the market reforms known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/doi-moi">Đổi Mới</a> in 1986. In 1988 it became the only political party when it absorbed its two sister parties, the Socialist Party and the Democratic Party.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The party is organised on the principle of democratic centralism. Its National Congress, held every five years, elects a Central Committee, which in turn elects the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/politburo">Politburo</a> and the Secretariat and appoints the general secretary, the highest party office. The supremacy of the party is guaranteed by Article 4 of the constitution. The party holds to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism-leninism">Marxism-Leninism</a> and to Ho Chi Minh Thought, which became its official ideologies in 1991. At its 10th National Congress in 2006 it amended its statutes to let members engage in private business. It describes its economic system as a socialist-oriented market economy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Communist Party of Vietnam” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>For a year the south was formally administered by the Republic of South Vietnam, the state of the Provisional Revolutionary Government, but real power lay with the <b>Vietnam Workers’ Party</b> in Hanoi, which decided on rapid reunification rather than a long transition.</blockquote><a href="/empire/socialist-republic-of-vietnam">Socialist Republic of Vietnam · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
